<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Alexey Kaledin</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1861">1861</a>–<a href="/years/1918">1918</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Alexey Kaledin was a Russian cavalry general and the first elected ataman of the Don <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cossacks">Cossacks</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/february-revolution">February Revolution</a> of 1917. A successful army commander in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, he refused to recognise the Bolshevik seizure of power and gave refuge to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volunteer-army">Volunteer Army</a> on the Don. As Cossacks deserted and Red forces advanced, he shot himself in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/novocherkassk">Novocherkassk</a> in February 1918.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Alexey Maksimovich Kaledin attended the Mikhaylovskoye Artillery School and the General Staff Academy. Early in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> he served as a cavalry commander, then took over a cavalry corps and rose to the rank of General of the Cavalry. On 5 April 1916 (23 March in the old calendar) he became commander of the 8th Army, part of the Imperial Russian Army's Southwestern Front. He took part in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brusilov-offensive">Brusilov Offensive</a> from June to September 1916 and won the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lutsk">Lutsk</a> in June of that year.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>At the Moscow State Conference of August 1917 Kaledin declared that all soviets and committees must be abolished, both in the army and in the rear. After the Kornilov affair he withdrew to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/novocherkassk">Novocherkassk</a> and the protection of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cossacks">Cossack</a> host, to avoid arrest by the Provisional Government. On 1 July 1917 (18 June in the old calendar) the Great Military Assembly of the Don Cossack Host elected him ataman. According to the historian Peter Kenez, he thus became the first democratically chosen leader of the Cossacks since 1723.</p><p>On 9 November, on hearing of the revolution in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petrograd">Petrograd</a>, Kaledin invited the members of the Provisional Government to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/novocherkassk">Novocherkassk</a> to join him in organising the anti-Bolshevik struggle. Mikhail Alekseyev arrived on 15 November and began forming a new army, and on 20 November the host declared its independence. Between 9 and 15 December Kaledin, aided by the Alekseyev Organization, suppressed Bolshevik resistance in Rostov-on-Don. Lavr Kornilov arrived on 19 December and by the end of the month had reorganised the Alekseyev Organization as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volunteer-army">Volunteer Army</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>By January 1918 Vladimir Antonov-Ovseenko was threatening <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rostov">Rostov</a> and Taganrog with a force led by Sivers, who took Taganrog on 10 February (28 January in the old calendar). Kornilov decided to retreat from the Don. Kaledin concluded that the situation was hopeless, resigned his post and shot himself on 11 February 1918 (29 January in the old calendar). His honours included the Order of St. George, 4th class for the fighting near Hnyla Lypa in October 1914 and 3rd class for the Battle of Kalush in September 1915.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Alexey Kaledin” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After Ataman <b>Alexey Kaledin</b> shot himself in February 1918 as Bolsheviks overran the Don, a Cossack uprising in spring drove them out.</blockquote><a href="/state/don-republic">Don Republic</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Ust-Khopyorskaya, in what is now <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/novocherkassk">Novocherkassk</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=49.57,42.4&amp;year=1898" aria-label="Ust-Khopyorskaya on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5525 437 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Alexey Kaledin"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5975 707h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5975 707h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=49.57,42.4&amp;year=1898">What was here in 1898? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
